% ============================================================================
% cell-division-daughter-cells — the two named eukaryotic cell-division
% processes and how many daughter cells each one produces
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Mitosis is already tabled here in detail (`mitosis-phases.adj`, its phase
% order, its ordinal-position bridge), but nowhere yet does this stdlib state
% the single headline number that distinguishes mitosis from its sister
% process, MEIOSIS: how many daughter cells each one actually produces. This
% is a genuinely NEW library, not an extension of `mitosis-phases.adj` --
% meiosis is a wholly different biological process (the one that makes
% gametes), not another phase or row of mitosis. A BIOLOGY / genetics agent
% reasons UP from this rung: before you can explain why a sperm and egg cell
% each carry half the usual chromosome number, or why mitosis keeps a cell
% line genetically identical across every ordinary division, you must first
% know HOW MANY cells each process yields. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? cell_division_daughter_cells(mitosis, $Count)   % 2
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a numeric-cell table like
% `geology/mineral-hardness.adj`): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `cell_division_daughter_cells(process, count)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the count WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any process that is not one of these two (it never
% invents a count).
%
% Both processes and the daughter-cell count the source states for each, as a
% little truth table:
%
%     process    daughter cells   a beginner's cue
%     --------   --------------   -----------------------------------------
%     mitosis    2                one diploid cell -> two identical cells
%     meiosis    4                one diploid cell -> four haploid gametes
%
% Because `count` is a NUMBER (not a label), a recalled value composes
% straight into a comparison: meiosis yields MORE daughter cells than mitosis
% exactly when count(meiosis) > count(mitosis) — the concrete bridge from
% RECALL to COMPUTE, the same property `mineral-hardness.adj` established for
% its own numeric cells. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a count and
% recall the process that produces it:
%
%     ? cell_division_daughter_cells($Process, 4)   % meiosis
%
% A word that is NOT one of these two named eukaryotic cell-division
% processes -- `binary_fission` (a real process, but the one PROKARYOTES /
% bacteria use to divide, not a eukaryotic process this table names at all)
% -- is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than
% inventing a count. That honest-abstention property is what makes the table
% safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the process->count pairs it
% can ground, and only those.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — both counts below were
% read out of fetched NIH National Human Genome Research Institute (NHGRI)
% "Genetics Glossary" pages this session, and either pair whose count could
% not be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair, with the
% verbatim sentence that states it (each sentence's OTHER clause -- "have
% identical genomes" for mitosis, "haploid... the gametes" for meiosis -- is
% quoted in full for honesty but is NOT what this table extracts; only the
% single daughter-cell COUNT each sentence states is tabled):
%
%   mitosis → 2
%     https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mitosis
%     (NIH National Human Genome Research Institute — Genetics Glossary)
%     "Mitosis is generally followed by equal division of the cell's content
%      into two daughter cells that have identical genomes."
%
%   meiosis → 4
%     https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Meiosis
%     (NIH National Human Genome Research Institute — Genetics Glossary)
%     "During meiosis, each diploid cell undergoes two rounds of division to
%      yield four haploid daughter cells — the gametes."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NHGRI mitosis sentence
% that fixes the first row (mitosis → 2). The OTHER row's own distinct
% citation is documented above, so each count remains independently
% checkable. Both sources are the same NIH NHGRI Genetics Glossary
% (genome.gov), already the trust tier `dna-base-pairs.adj` and
% `anatomy/body-counts.adj` establish in this stdlib, a primary U.S.
% government reference, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table cell_division_daughter_cells {
    columns process, count

    row (mitosis, 2)
    row (meiosis, 4)

    source "Mitosis is generally followed by equal division of the cell's content into two daughter cells that have identical genomes."
    locator "https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mitosis"
    trust authoritative
}
